#. Tag: title
#: faq.docbook:5
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kpilot; Startup Problems"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:11
#, no-c-format
msgid "What do I put after <option>--debug</option>?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:16
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Nothing. Most versions of &kpilot; don't even have a <option>--debug</"
"option> option, and will complain if you use it. For those versions that "
"<emphasis>do</emphasis> have it, use a value between 1 and 4, which will "
"control the amount of debugging printed (a little). Using a value of 1 will "
"print a fairly complete call trace without the really-boring functions, "
"while 4 will include every trivial function in all of &kpilot;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:32
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kpilot; says <errorname>Can't connect to pilot</errorname>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:37
#, no-c-format
msgid "This can have various causes. Check that:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:41
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The pilot device (usually <filename class=\"devicefile\">/dev/pilot</"
"filename>) exists and points to the serial port the &PalmPilot; is actually "
"connected to."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:46
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To link the &PalmPilot; device to the correct serial port, you can either "
"fill in <filename class=\"devicefile\">/dev/ttyS<replaceable>n</"
"replaceable></filename> in the <guilabel>Pilot Device</guilabel> field in "
"the <link linkend=\"page-general\">setup dialog</link> or (preferably) link "
"<filename class=\"devicefile\">/dev/pilot</filename> to <filename class="
"\"devicefile\">/dev/ttyS<replaceable>n</replaceable></filename> with the "
"following command (as <systemitem class=\"username\">root</systemitem>): "
"<userinput> <command>ln</command> <option>-s</option> <parameter>/dev/"
"ttyS<replaceable>n</replaceable></parameter> <parameter>/dev/pilot</"
"parameter></userinput> Here <filename class=\"devicefile\">/dev/"
"ttyS<replaceable>n</replaceable></filename> is the name of the serial port; "
"replace <replaceable>n</replaceable> with the correct number (usually 0 or "
"1)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:71
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Check that you have permission to read and write to the serial port. The "
"permissions for the serial port should be such that you can write to it. "
"This is most easily done by running the following (as root): <userinput> "
"<command>chmod</command> <option>666</option> <parameter>/dev/"
"ttyS<replaceable>n</replaceable></parameter> </userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:83
#, no-c-format
msgid "Try starting the daemon by hand before starting &kpilot;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:88
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"(For &Linux-Mandrake; 7 systems) Check the system security level: settings "
"higher than 3 prevent some forms of inter-process communication which are "
"necessary for &kpilot; to operate correctly."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: faq.docbook:95
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"(<emphasis>&Linux-Mandrake; security information courtesy of Jay Summett</"
"emphasis>) To set your <acronym>MSEC</acronym> (Mandrake SECurity) settings "
"to not block the &kpilot; socket (for connections to localhost) you can "
"login as root and type <userinput> <command>/etc/security/msec/init.sh</"
"command> <option>3</option> </userinput> Which will set your <acronym>MSEC</"
"acronym> level to 3 (regular security). For more information about the "
"various security levels, &etc;, see: <ulink url=\"http://www.linux-mandrake."
"com/userguide/en/reference/017.html#157\"> the Mandrake reference guide</"
"ulink>."
msgstr ""

